#!/usr/bin/env bash
# JankyBorders draws the focus ring macOS does not. Apple's only cue is a
# slightly lighter title bar, which disappears the moment two windows of the
# same app overlap; this says plainly which one has the keyboard.
#
# borders reads and executes this file when it starts with no arguments. A
# later run against a live process updates the border in place, so after an
# edit just run this file again - no restart, no rebuild.
#
# width is DOUBLE the ring you actually see. JankyBorders strokes the window
# frame centred, so the inner half of the stroke sits behind the window and
# only the outer half is visible. Measured on screen: width=20 paints x=18..27
# against a window whose content starts at x=28, i.e. exactly half. 10.0 here
# is the 5px ring the paragraph below is about - do not "correct" it to 5.
#
# Colours are Catppuccin Mocha (blue + overlay0) rather than the rose-pine moon
# iris the rest of this machine uses: at width 5 the focus ring is furniture,
# not syntax, so it reads better as a cold accent than as another palette
# colour. Catppuccin's blue is bright without being saturated, which is what
# makes a ring this thick bearable to look at all day. Format is 0xAARRGGBB.
# Full option list: https://github.com/FelixKratz/JankyBorders
# (nixpkgs' jankyborders ships the binary only - there is no man page here.)

options=(
	style=round
	width=10.0   # a 5px ring; see the note above
	# This machine drives a 1920x1080 display at 1:1, so retina drawing is off.
	# (This makes no measurable difference to the stroke either way.)
	hidpi=off
	active_color=0xff89b4fa   # catppuccin mocha blue
	inactive_color=0xff6c7086 # catppuccin mocha overlay0
)

borders "${options[@]}"
